<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Mitla</h1><p class="st-meta">900 CE – 1521 CE (approx.) · capital Mitla</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The great Postclassic Zapotec religious centre of the Valley of Oaxaca, famed for the stone fretwork mosaics on its palaces.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Mitla, Lyobaa in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zapotec-civilization">Zapotec</a>, was occupied for many centuries, but it grew into a major centre after the decline of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/monte-alban">Monte Albán</a>, from around 900. Spanish sources describe it as the seat of the Zapotec high priest and a place of burial for kings.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its palace complexes are decorated with intricate geometric step-fret mosaics made of thousands of cut stones fitted without mortar, unlike anything elsewhere in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>. Cruciform tombs lie beneath the courts. Mitla served as a religious centre and was linked politically with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zaachila">Zapotec kingdom of Zaachila</a>, while sharing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mixtecs">Mixtec</a> artistic influences.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Mitla came under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a> in the late 15th century and was taken by the Spanish around 1521. The Church of San Pablo was built over one of its palaces. It remains a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zapotec-civilization">Zapotec</a> town.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/mitla.jpg" alt="Mitla" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Uploaded into English Wikipedia by Bobak Ha'Eri · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:12-05oaxaca076.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>900 CE – 1521 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mitla</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Zapotec religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Zapotec</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lyobaa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1210&amp;focus=mitla" aria-label="Mitla on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2058 1549 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Mitla lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2378 1805L2321 1717"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2321 1717h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2378 1805h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2378 1805h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1210&amp;focus=mitla">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
